## Finding

`cnstaging-waf-policy-v2` is **WAF + rate limiting only, with a default `allow` for `*`**. It blocks attack signatures but not unknown users, so `/ai-test/*` is currently open to the whole internet. Option B (dedicated allowlist policy) is needed.

⚠️ Also, the OWASP rules may block legitimate AI prompts (SQLi/XSS/RCE signatures can false-positive on code or free text). A dedicated policy avoids that.

## Create the policy (GUI)

1. Network security → Cloud Armor policies → **Create policy**.
2. Name `ai-test-allowlist`, Type **Backend security policy**, Default rule action **Deny (403)**.
3. Add rule: priority `1000`, Action **Allow**, Match source IPs `110.227.205.102, 14.99.145.222` ← required substitution. Confirm these are your current office/VPN IPs (taken from `temp-testing-cn`).
4. Targets: add `ai-test-backend` (this replaces `cnstaging-waf-policy-v2` on that backend only; staging backends are untouched).
5. Create.

Cost: about $5/mo plus about $1/mo per rule, which is about $6/mo until you delete it.

